"""One-off: replace the longest skill descriptions with shorter ones that keep the
trigger situations and the skip pointers. Run from the repo root."""
import re

NEW = {
"data-engineering": "Builds and fixes data pipelines into a warehouse or lake so the numbers can be trusted. Use for ETL or ELT jobs, duplicates after a rerun, warehouse totals that do not match the source, backfills, incremental loads, late data, schema drift and data quality checks (Airflow, dbt, Spark, BigQuery, Snowflake). Skip for training or evaluating models (data-ml-work) and for app database schema changes (schema-changes).",
"infrastructure-as-code": "Writes and changes cloud infrastructure as code (Terraform, OpenTofu, Pulumi, CloudFormation, CDK, Bicep) without destroying production or leaking secrets. Use for terraform plan and apply, state files, drift, importing console-built resources and splitting environments. Skip for app deploy pipelines (release-engineering), cloud bills (cloud-cost) and choosing architectures (design-decision).",
"cloud-cost": "Finds why a cloud bill is high or jumped and cuts it safely. Use for rising AWS, Azure or GCP bills, idle or oversized resources, NAT and egress costs, savings plans or reserved instances, budgets and cost estimates. Reads the bill breakdown first, takes safe savings, commits only after right-sizing. Skip for making code faster (performance-work) and for writing the infrastructure code (infrastructure-as-code).",
"ux-research": "Finds out what users need and whether a design works, before or after building. Use for user interviews, surveys, usability tests, personas, whether people want a feature, why trial users do not convert, and reading feedback. Skip for visual design (frontend-design), polishing a built screen (ui-polish) and deciding requirements with a stakeholder (requirements-interview).",
"git-hygiene": "Keeps git history clean and recovers from git mistakes without losing work. Use for squashing or rewriting commits, rebase versus merge, undoing a commit or push, lost changes, a pushed secret or large file, force pushes, commit messages and branching rules. Makes a backup before any history change and never force-pushes a shared branch without a named yes. Skip for deploy pipelines (release-engineering) and reviewing a change (change-review).",
"data-ml-work": "Does machine learning and data analysis work you can trust. Use for training or evaluating a model, accuracy that looks too good, leakage, train and test splits, baselines, metrics, forecasting, A/B test readouts and analysing a dataset. Checks the question, the data and a simple baseline before modelling. Skip for plain app code (new-app-build), pipelines into a warehouse (data-engineering) and designing a user study (ux-research).",
"mobile-app-work": "Builds and ships iOS and Android apps without store and device surprises. Use for React Native, Flutter, Expo, Swift or Kotlin work, App Store or Play Store submission and rejections, push notifications, permissions, offline mode, forcing updates and bugs that only show on real phones. Skip for responsive websites (web-quality) and the backend API alone (api-design).",
"dependency-audit": "Audits third-party dependencies for known vulnerabilities, licence problems, abandoned packages and risky update habits, then fixes what matters in safe steps. Use for npm audit noise, licence questions such as GPL in closed source, outdated packages, Dependabot or Renovate setup, SBOMs and whether a package is safe to add. Skip for upgrading one major framework (migrate-and-upgrade) and reviewing the project's own code (security-review).",
"accessibility-review": "Audits a product against WCAG and tests it the way disabled people use it, reporting findings by who is blocked and how to fix each. Use for accessibility audits, VPATs, ADA, Section 508 or EAA compliance, screen reader and keyboard-only checks, colour blindness and accessibility complaints. Skip for quick fixes while building a page (web-quality) and visual polish (ui-polish).",
"api-design": "Designs and changes HTTP, GraphQL or library APIs so callers do not get broken. Use for designing endpoints, REST or GraphQL, response shapes, errors, pagination, naming, OpenAPI specs, versioning, deprecation and whether a change is breaking. Writes the contract with examples before code. Skip for choosing whole architectures (design-decision) and internal functions with one caller.",
"security-prompter": "Asks the security questions only the user can answer, before or while something is built, and turns the answers into security requirements. Use when starting an app, feature, API or integration that handles user data, logins, payments, files, admin access or the internet, or for threat models and security checklists. Skip for auditing finished code (security-review) and tasks with no data or exposure.",
"codebase-onboarding": "Learns an unfamiliar codebase quickly and writes a short verified CLAUDE.md. Use when the user just joined or inherited a repo, asks how a codebase works, where to start, how to run it, or to write a CLAUDE.md. Reads CI and build files first, checks commands, traces one real journey, notes conventions and danger zones. Skip for changing a known codebase (coding-discipline) and designing something new (new-app-build).",
"estimation": "Estimates how long software work will take and how sure the estimate is. Use for how long will this take, can we ship by a date, timelines, story points, sizing tickets, what fits in a quarter and scope versus deadline. Gives a range with assumptions and the unknowns that dominate risk. Skip for deciding what to build (requirements-interview) and the build itself (new-app-build).",
"payments-integration": "Adds payments and subscriptions with Stripe or similar without losing money or storing card data. Use for checkout, subscriptions, failed renewals, webhooks, double charges, refunds, invoices and test versus live mode. Keeps card data off your server, grants access from verified webhooks and makes handlers safe to repeat. Skip for deciding what to charge and for general API design (api-design).",
"launch-gate": "Runs a go or no-go check before a first public release or big launch and gives a verdict with named owners for open items. Use for are we ready to launch, go-live checklists, pre-launch reviews and what could go wrong on launch day. Checks a real payment, a restored backup, alerts, rollback and on-call. Skip for routine releases (release-engineering) and live outages (incident-response).",
"observability": "Makes a running system easy to understand and alert on, with logs, metrics, traces, dashboards and alerts. Use for adding logging or monitoring, finding out about problems from customers, noisy or missing alerts, SLOs, error budgets and tracing. Starts from the questions asked during an outage and ties every alert to an action. Skip for debugging one known bug (bug-investigation) and a live outage (incident-response).",
"incident-response": "Guides the handling of a live production incident and the review afterwards. Use when production is down or erroring, the site is slow right now, something broke after a deploy, someone got paged, or for postmortems. Stops the harm before finding the cause, keeps a timeline, waits for a yes before touching production. Skip for bugs that are not hurting users now (bug-investigation).",
"performance-work": "Makes slow software faster by measuring, finding the real bottleneck and proving the gain. Use for slow endpoints or queries, high CPU, memory leaks, latency, load capacity and profiling. Sets a target, takes a baseline and fixes the biggest cost first. Skip for slow web pages in the browser (web-quality) and tiny scripts that run once.",
"schema-changes": "Plans and runs database schema changes on live tables without downtime. Use for adding, renaming or dropping columns or tables, changing types, adding indexes, backfilling data and writing migrations when the app must stay up. Uses expand and contract steps and tests on production-sized data. Never runs a migration on real data without a yes. Skip for local throwaway databases and rollout planning (release-engineering).",
}

for name, desc in NEW.items():
    assert ': ' not in desc, name
    p = f'skills/{name}/SKILL.md'
    s = open(p, encoding='utf-8').read()
    s2, n = re.subn(r'^description:.*$', 'description: ' + desc.replace('\\', '\\\\'), s, count=1, flags=re.M)
    assert n == 1, name
    open(p, 'w', encoding='utf-8').write(s2)
print('updated', len(NEW))
